## Source
Audit 0221 (2026-06-04) decided ADOPT: 47 verified supersession events
across 3 stores (aedist 18, chemin-de-voix 26, git-erg 3), STOP-ADOPT
fired after the three largest stores. Full event list in 0221's log.
Cross-project recurrence proves the relation wants tooling support.

## Empirical patterns the design must cover (from the sweep)
- One-to-many: a ticket superseded by several (0153 -> 0199+0200;
  chemin 0222 -> 0226+0227).
- Batch many-to-one: 24 LoRA tickets all superseded by 0237+0238 in
  one mass closure (chemin-de-voix).
- Declared on either side or both: old ticket says "superseded by N",
  new ticket says "supersedes N" (aedist 0141<->0156, 0289<->0292).
- Out of scope: supersession by PR/commits/scripts/models with no
  ticket ID (git-erg 0112 names only PR #105) -- stays a log-line
  convention, not a header.

## Decisions (author, 2026-06-04)
- Direction: `Superseded-by:` on the CLOSED ticket -- the durable record
  lives where the sweep found it. No header on the new ticket.
- Repeatable: yes (one-to-many: a ticket superseded by several).
- check rules: referenced ID must exist (error, like Blocked-by
  cross-ref); ticket carrying Superseded-by still open = WARNING
  (transition window between filing the successor and closing the old).
- Self-reference rejected (error). No cycle-detection machinery --
  defer until a real cycle is ever observed.
- migrate retrofit: leave history untouched. Historical "superseded by
  NNNN" log lines stay as-is; the header is for new supersessions.
- No version bump: backward-compatible addition to the closed set,
  Label precedent (pep section 12). Magic line stays %erg 0.1.

## Spec questions to settle (settled above -- kept for the record)
- Header on which side? Sweep shows the durable record usually lives
  in the CLOSED ticket ("superseded by"); the header on the new ticket
  (Supersedes: NNNN) matches the one wild instance (aedist 0292) and
  is what the 0221 question proposed. Decide direction, or both.
- Repeatable? One-to-many and many-to-one patterns say yes (like
  Blocked-by).
- check rules: referenced ID must exist (like Blocked-by cross-ref);
  superseded target SHOULD be closed -- warning or error when open?
- Self-reference and cycles rejected (reuse Blocked-by DAG machinery?).
- migrate retrofit: recognize the documented log-line convention
  ("superseded by NNNN" in Closed:/log) -- decide whether migrate
  rewrites it into the header or leaves history untouched (0221 events
  are historical record; retrofitting may be wrong -- bias to leave).
- PEP note: X-Supersedes was explicitly rejected in the PR #385 era
  (open extension headers); adding Supersedes to the CLOSED set via
  version bump is the sanctioned path (pep-erg-v1.md section 2). Spec
  bump 0.1 -> 0.2 or document as backward-compatible addition like
  Label (pep section 12 precedent).

## Exit criteria
- [x] Spec section for Superseded-by: in spec-erg-v1.md (semantics,
      direction: closed ticket carries it, repeatable, validation tier)
- [x] erg check validates the cross-reference (unknown ID = error;
      carrier still open = warning; self-ref = error)
- [x] erg validate accepts the header; unknown-header rejection message
      no longer fires on it
- [x] migrate decision recorded: leave history untouched (decided
      2026-06-04, see log); no migrate.go change
- [x] PEP rationale section added (evidence: 0221 tally)
- [x] make check passes

## Plan

### Closed/archived-corpus decision

`loadErgs` (src/go/erg.go:668) does a full recursive `filepath.WalkDir`
of the ticket store, including `tickets/closed/`. Both `validateCorpus`
and `corpusWarnings` receive the complete slice. Therefore `allIDs` in
`validateCorpus` always contains IDs from closed and archived tickets.

**Decision**: The cross-ref check for `Superseded-by` uses the same
`allIDs` map already built for Blocked-by (validate.go:144-148).
A `Superseded-by` in a closed ticket referencing another closed or
archived ticket resolves without special handling.

**Archive guard**: `archive.go` guards only against archiving a ticket
that open tickets still `Blocked-by`-reference (i.e. a ticket that
blocks others). `Superseded-by` points FROM the old ticket TO its
replacements — the old ticket is the carrier, its refs are internal to
it. When the old (closed) ticket is archived, no open ticket references
it via `Superseded-by`. No archive guard needed.

**Self-ref check**: Implemented at parse time in the `Superseded-by`
case (erg.go), mirroring how cycle detection handles self-loops for
Blocked-by. A ref whose ID equals the ticket's own `FilenameID()`
is a parse-time error: `%s:%d: Superseded-by self-reference`.

### Actions

**Action 1 — ergspecv1.go: register header and add Erg fields**
File: `src/go/ergspecv1.go`
- Line 66-68: add `"Superseded-by": true` to `v1HeaderKeys`.
  `v1SingletonKeys` is NOT modified — the header is repeatable.
- Lines 24-38 (`Erg` struct): add two new fields after `BlockedBys`:
  ```
  SupersededBys     []Ref
  SupersededByLines []int
  ```

**Action 2 — erg.go: parse Superseded-by in the header switch**
File: `src/go/erg.go`
- In `parseErgBytes`, inside the header switch (after the `case
  "Blocked-by":` block, ~lines 378-384):
  ```go
  case "Superseded-by":
      ref, refErr := parseRef(val)
      if refErr != nil {
          errs = append(errs, fmt.Sprintf("%s:%d: %v", name, lineNum, refErr))
      } else if ref.Kind == RefLocal && ref.ID == strings.TrimSuffix(
          strings.SplitN(name, "-", 2)[0], "") {
          errs = append(errs, fmt.Sprintf(
              "%s:%d: Superseded-by self-reference", name, lineNum))
      }
      supersededBys = append(supersededBys, ref)
      supersededByLines = append(supersededByLines, lineNum)
  ```
  Note: the self-ref check compares `ref.ID` against the ticket's own
  4-digit prefix extracted from `name` (the basename). Extract via the
  same pattern as `FilenameID()`: `stem[:strings.Index(stem, "-")]`.
- Declare locals alongside `blockedBys`/`blockedByLines`:
  ```go
  var supersededBys []Ref
  var supersededByLines []int
  ```
- Add to the returned `Erg` struct at the bottom of `parseErgBytes`:
  ```
  SupersededBys:     supersededBys,
  SupersededByLines: supersededByLines,
  ```

**Action 3 — validate.go: add cross-ref rule for Superseded-by**
File: `src/go/validate.go`
- In `validateCorpus`, after the Rule 10 block (lines 152-162), add:
  ```go
  // Superseded-by cross-ref: referenced ID must exist in corpus.
  for i := range tickets {
      t := &tickets[i]
      name := t.Filename()
      for _, ref := range t.SupersededBys {
          corpusOpCount++
          if ref.Kind == RefLocal && !allIDs[ref.ID] {
              errors = append(errors, fmt.Sprintf(
                  "%s: Superseded-by '%s' references unknown ticket ID",
                  name, ref.ID))
          }
      }
  }
  ```
